thunderbolt: Add dp_max_bw parameter to cap DP tunnel bandwidth reservation

Without USB4 bandwidth allocation mode, a DP tunnel whose DPRX capability
read is still pending is accounted at the full DP IN/OUT capability (e.g.
HBR3 x4 = 25920 Mb/s). When two tunnels are created back to back (boot,
dock hotplug) the second one only gets the leftovers and is reduced, e.g.
to HBR x4 = 8640 Mb/s, which is not enough for 4K@60. The reduction is
never revisited after the first tunnel settles at its real DPRX rate.

dp_max_bw (Mb/s, 0 = no limit) caps what a single new tunnel may reserve
so that several tunnels can share the link independently of creation order.

--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
@@ -43,6 +43,11 @@
 
 #define MAX_GROUPS		7	/* max Group_ID is 7 */
 
+static int dp_max_bw;
+module_param(dp_max_bw, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(dp_max_bw,
+		 "maximum bandwidth (Mb/s) a new DP tunnel may reserve, 0 = no limit (default: 0)");
+
 static unsigned int asym_threshold = TB_ASYM_THRESHOLD;
 module_param_named(asym_threshold, asym_threshold, uint, 0444);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(asym_threshold,
@@ -2034,6 +2039,19 @@
 		goto err_reclaim_usb;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Without bandwidth allocation mode, a tunnel whose DPRX read is
+	 * still pending is accounted at the full DP IN/OUT capability, so
+	 * a tunnel created right after it gets only the leftovers. Cap the
+	 * reservation so several tunnels can share the link fairly.
+	 */
+	if (dp_max_bw > 0) {
+		if (available_up > dp_max_bw)
+			available_up = dp_max_bw;
+		if (available_down > dp_max_bw)
+			available_down = dp_max_bw;
+	}
+
 	tb_dbg(tb, "available bandwidth for new DP tunnel %u/%u Mb/s\n",
 	       available_up, available_down);
 
